      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The yellowing leaves on the lower stems of your tomato plants, a classic case of \"gollum's favorite leaf\" - you know, the one that's always getting left behind, neglected and forgotten. But don't worry, it's not a goblin's favorite snack, it's just a sign of a problem that can be easily fixed.\n\nThe most likely culprit is root bound-ness, where the plant has outgrown its container and needs more room to breathe. It's like when a group of goblins in a small cave start to feel cramped and claustrophobic, they need to expand their territory to accommodate their growing numbers. To fix this, you can try transplanting the plant into a larger container with fresh, well-draining soil.\n\nAnother possibility is nutrient deficiency, perhaps a lack of magnesium or potassium. You can try adding a balanced fertilizer to the soil to give your plants a boost. Just be careful not to overdo it, or you'll end up with a plant that's as weak as a goblin's grip on a slippery rock.\n\nLastly, it's also possible that the plant is experiencing a fungal disease, like root rot or leaf spot. In this case, you'll need to take a more",
